    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Enthroned at seven and ruling through regents until 1669, the Kangxi Emperor reigned for 61 years, the longest reign of any Chinese emperor.</li><li>He put down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Revolt of the Three Feudatories</a> in 1681, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1683, agreed a border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at Nerchinsk in 1689 and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, with severe measures against some of those who resisted.</li><li>His later years were marked by a succession dispute among his sons, and historians still debate whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> took the throne legitimately.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Thomas Pereira taught the Kangxi Emperor to play the harpsichord, and he was the first Chinese emperor to play a western musical instrument.</p></aside>
    </section>

